--TEST--
Core\Crypto::open under the case a message was not sealed with is refused at every length with the same sentence a wrong key earns, and never with plausible bytes — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A sealed message carries no label saying which construction wrote it, so the
// only thing stopping `Aes256Gcm` from reading an XChaCha20-Poly1305 message as
// `nonce(12) ‖ body` is the tag. This is the case that says it does, in both
// directions and at every length.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();

// A sweep rather than one length, counted rather than read off a line: the two
// nonce widths differ by 12 octets, so a length at which the layouts happen to
// line up is exactly what a single example would miss.
array<uint> $lengths = [0, 1, 12, 16, 24, 28, 40, 64, 257];

string $sentence = "";
int $tried = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $sameSentence = 0;
foreach ($lengths as uint $length) {
    bytes $message = Core\Bytes::fill($length, 65);

    bytes $extended = Core\Crypto::seal($message, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    $tried += 1;
    try {
        bytes $asInterop = Core\Crypto::open($extended, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm);
    } catch (RuntimeError $notThisCipher) {
        $refused += 1;
        if ($sentence == "") {
            $sentence = $notThisCipher->message;
        }
        if ($notThisCipher->message == $sentence) {
            $sameSentence += 1;
        }
    }

    bytes $interop = Core\Crypto::seal($message, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm);
    $tried += 1;
    try {
        bytes $asExtended = Core\Crypto::open($interop, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::XChaCha20Poly1305);
    } catch (RuntimeError $stillNotThisCipher) {
        $refused += 1;
        if ($stillNotThisCipher->message == $sentence) {
            $sameSentence += 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "refused ", $refused, " of ", $tried, "\n";
echo "one sentence ", $sameSentence, " of ", $refused, "\n";

// The sentence itself, and it is the one a wrong key earns rather than a
// second one: a caller that could tell "wrong cipher" from "wrong key" apart
// would learn which half of a forgery attempt landed.
bytes $probe = Core\Crypto::seal("attack at dawn" as bytes, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm);
secret bytes $other = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
try {
    bytes $wrongKey = Core\Crypto::open($probe, $other, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm);
    echo "opened under the wrong key", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $notThisKey) {
    if ($notThisKey->message == $sentence) {
        echo "the wrong key earns the same sentence", "\n";
    } else {
        echo "a second sentence", "\n";
    }
}
echo $sentence, "\n";

// The other side of the bound: the case it was sealed under still opens it, so
// the refusals above are about the cipher named and not about `open` having
// become a member that refuses everything.
if (Core\Crypto::open($probe, $key, Core\Crypto\Cipher::Aes256Gcm) == "attack at dawn" as bytes) {
    echo "and the case it was sealed under opens it", "\n";
} else {
    echo "refused its own message", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
refused 18 of 18
one sentence 18 of 18
the wrong key earns the same sentence
Core\Crypto::open(): $sealed is not an authentic message under $key — it has been altered, it is too short to be one, or the key is not the one it was sealed under
and the case it was sealed under opens it
